<div class="ltx_page_main">
<div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line" lang="en">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">The relative canonical resolution: <span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Macaulay2</span>-package, experiments and conjectures</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Christian Bopp
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Michael Hoff
</span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">This short note provides a quick introduction to relative canonical resolutions of curves on rational normal scrolls. We present our <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Macaulay2</span>-package which computes the relative canonical resolution associated to a curve and a pencil of divisors. Most of our experimental data can be found on the following webpage <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">BBH18</a>]</cite>. We end with a list of conjectural shapes of relative canonical resolutions. In particular, for curves of genus <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=n\cdot k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=n\cdot k+1</annotation></semantics></math> and pencils of degree <math id="abstract1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="abstract1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, we conjecture that the syzygy divisors on the Hurwitz scheme <math id="abstract1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{H}_{g,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{H}_{g,k}</annotation></semantics></math> constructed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">DP18</a>]</cite> all have the same support.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Relative Canonical Resolutions</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <em id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">relative canonical resolution</em> is the minimal free resolution of a canonically embedded curve <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> inside a rational normal scroll. Every such scroll is swept out by linear spaces parametized by pencils of divisors on <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Studying divisors on moduli spaces reveals certain aspects of the global geometry of these spaces.
A famous example for curves of odd genus <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is the <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Koszul-divisor</em> on the moduli space of curves <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is induced by the minimal free resolution of <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset\mathbb{P}^{g-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset\mathbb{P}^{g-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Set-theoretically the Koszul-divisor consists of curves, such that the minimal free resolution of the canonical model has extra-syzygies at a certain step.
In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">BP15</a>]</cite>,<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">DP15</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">DP18</a>]</cite>, the relative canonical resolution was used to define similar <em id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">syzygy divisors</em> on Hurwitz spaces <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{H}_{g,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{H}_{g,k}</annotation></semantics></math>, parametizing pairs of curves of genus <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and pencils of divisors of degree <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> (equivalently, covers of <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="S1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> by curves of genus <math id="S1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We will briefly summarize the connections between pencils of divisors on canonical curves and
rational normal scrolls in order to define the relative canonical resolution.
Let <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset\mathbb{P}^{g-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset\mathbb{P}^{g-1}</annotation></semantics></math> be a canonically embedded curve of genus <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> and let further</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{1}_{k}=\{D_{\lambda}\}_{\lambda\in\mathbb{P}^{1}}\subset|D|" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{1}_{k}=\{D_{\lambda}\}_{\lambda\in\mathbb{P}^{1}}\subset|D|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">be a pencil of divisors of degree <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.
If we denote by <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{D_{\lambda}}\subset\mathbb{P}^{g-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{D_{\lambda}}\subset\mathbb{P}^{g-1}</annotation></semantics></math> the linear span of the divisor, then</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=\bigcup_{\lambda\in\mathbb{P}^{1}}\overline{D_{\lambda}}\subset\mathbb{P}^{g-1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></munder><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=\bigcup_{\lambda\in\mathbb{P}^{1}}\overline{D_{\lambda}}\subset\mathbb{P}^{g-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">is a <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k-1)</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional rational normal scroll of degree <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=g-k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=g-k+1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1}\geq e_{2}\geq\dots\geq e_{d}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1}\geq e_{2}\geq\dots\geq e_{d}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> be integers,
<math id="S1.Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{E}=\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(e_{1})\oplus\dots\oplus\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(e_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{E}=\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(e_{1})\oplus\dots\oplus\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(e_{d})</annotation></semantics></math> and let
<math id="S1.Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:{\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})}\to\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:{\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})}\to\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be the corresponding <math id="S1.Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{d-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{d-1}</annotation></semantics></math>-bundle.
<br class="ltx_break">A <em id="S1.Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">rational normal scroll</em> <math id="S1.Thmdefinition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=S(e_{1},\dots,e_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=S(e_{1},\dots,e_{d})</annotation></semantics></math> of type <math id="S1.Thmdefinition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(e_{1},\dots,e_{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(e_{1},\dots,e_{d})</annotation></semantics></math> is the image of</p>
<table id="S1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j:\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})\to\mathbb{P}H^{0}({\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})},\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})}(1))=\mathbb{P}^{r}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j:\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})\to\mathbb{P}H^{0}({\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})},\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})}(1))=\mathbb{P}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmdefinition1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.Thmdefinition1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=f+d-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=f+d-1</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S1.Thmdefinition1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f=e_{1}+\dots+e_{d}\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f=e_{1}+\dots+e_{d}\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Conversely if <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is a rational normal scroll of degree <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> containing a canonical curve,
then the ruling on <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> cuts out a pencil of divisors <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{D_{\lambda}\}\subset|D|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>λ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{D_{\lambda}\}\subset|D|</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{0}(C,\omega_{C}\otimes\mathscr{O}_{C}(D)^{-1})=f." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{0}(C,\omega_{C}\otimes\mathscr{O}_{C}(D)^{-1})=f.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider a non-hyperelliptic canonically embedded curve <math id="S1.Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset\mathbb{P}^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset\mathbb{P}^{3}</annotation></semantics></math> of genus <math id="S1.Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>. The curve <math id="S1.Thmdefinition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is a complete intersection of a quadric surface <math id="S1.Thmdefinition2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> and a cubic surface <math id="S1.Thmdefinition2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S1.Thmdefinition2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> admits exactly two pencils of degree <math id="S1.Thmdefinition2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> (which is also the maximal number), then the quadric <math id="S1.Thmdefinition2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to <math id="S1.Thmdefinition2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}\times\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}\times\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. By Bézouts theorem, the two rulings of lines on <math id="S1.Thmdefinition2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> cut out the two pencils of degree <math id="S1.Thmdefinition2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S1.Thmdefinition2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and conversely, the quadric is the scroll of type <math id="S1.Thmdefinition2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1,1)</annotation></semantics></math> swept out by any of these pencils. If <math id="S1.Thmdefinition2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> only admits one pencil of degree <math id="S1.Thmdefinition2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>, then the quadric <math id="S1.Thmdefinition2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to a cone (i.e., a quadric of rank <math id="S1.Thmdefinition2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>) and coincides with the scroll of type <math id="S1.Thmdefinition2.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,2)</annotation></semantics></math> swept out by the unique pencil.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Har81</a>]</cite> it is shown that the variety <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> defined above is a non-degenerate <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional variety of minimal degree
<math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg X=f=r-d+1=\text{{codim}}X+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>codim</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg X=f=r-d+1=\text{{codim}}X+1</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{1},\dots,e_{d}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{1},\dots,e_{d}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j:\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})\to X\subset\mathbb{P}H^{0}({\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})},\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})}(1))=\mathbb{P}^{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j:\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})\to X\subset\mathbb{P}H^{0}({\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})},\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})}(1))=\mathbb{P}^{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is an isomorphism. Otherwise, it is a resolution of singularities. Since <math id="S1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{i}j_{*}\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{i}j_{*}\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, it is convenient to consider <math id="S1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})}</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="S1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> for cohomological considerations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">It is furthermore known, that the Picard group <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{{Pic}}({\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Pic</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{{Pic}}({\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})})</annotation></semantics></math> is generated
by the ruling <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=[\pi^{*}\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(1)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=[\pi^{*}\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(1)]</annotation></semantics></math> and the hyperplane class
<math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H=[j^{*}\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{r}}(1)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>j</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mi>r</mi></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H=[j^{*}\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{r}}(1)]</annotation></semantics></math> with intersection products</p>
<table id="S1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{d}=f,\ \ H^{d-1}\cdot R=1,\ \ R^{2}=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{d}=f,\ \ H^{d-1}\cdot R=1,\ \ R^{2}=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p6.2" class="ltx_p">Hence, we will write a line bundle <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})}(aH+bR)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})}(aH+bR)</annotation></semantics></math> in the following form</p>
<table id="S1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})}(aH+bR)=\pi^{*}(\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(b))(aH)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})}(aH+bR)=\pi^{*}(\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(b))(aH).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmdefinition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sch86</a>]</cite>, Corollary 4.4)<span id="S1.Thmdefinition3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> be a curve with a complete base point free <math id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{1}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{1}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and
let <math id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})}</annotation></semantics></math> be the projective bundle associated to the scroll <math id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, swept out by the <math id="S1.Thmdefinition3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{1}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{1}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(a)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset{\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset{\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has a resolution </span><math id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bullet}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bullet}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of type</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to\pi^{*}N_{k-2}(-kH)\to\pi^{*}N_{k-3}((-k+2)H)\to\dots\to\pi^{*}N_{1}(-2H)\to\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})}\to\mathscr{O}_{C}\to 0" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to\pi^{*}N_{k-2}(-kH)\to\pi^{*}N_{k-3}((-k+2)H)\to\dots\to\pi^{*}N_{1}(-2H)\to\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})}\to\mathscr{O}_{C}\to 0</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with </span><math id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N_{i}=\bigoplus_{j=1}^{\beta_{i}}\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(a_{j}^{(i)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N_{i}=\bigoplus_{j=1}^{\beta_{i}}\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(a_{j}^{(i)})</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\beta_{i}=\frac{i(k-2-i)}{k-1}\binom{k}{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\beta_{i}=\frac{i(k-2-i)}{k-1}\binom{k}{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.ix1.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(b)</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The complex </span><math id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bullet}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bullet}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is self dual, i.e.,
</span><math id="S1.I1.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{H}\!om(F_{\bullet},\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})}(-kH+(f-2)R))\cong F_{\bullet}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.830em;" width="0.830em"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℋ</mi></mpadded><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{H}\!om(F_{\bullet},\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})}(-kH+(f-2)R))\cong F_{\bullet}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The resolution <math id="S1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bullet}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bullet}</annotation></semantics></math> above is called the <em id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">relative canonical resolution</em>.
The degree of the bundles <math id="S1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> was computed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">BH15b</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1.4</span></span><span id="S1.Thmdefinition4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">BH15b</a>]</cite>, Proposition 2.9)<span id="S1.Thmdefinition4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmdefinition4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The degree of the bundle <math id="S1.Thmdefinition4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of rank <math id="S1.Thmdefinition4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta_{i}=\frac{k}{i+1}(k-2-i)\binom{k-2}{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta_{i}=\frac{k}{i+1}(k-2-i)\binom{k-2}{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math>
in the relative canonical resolution <math id="S1.Thmdefinition4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F_{\bullet}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>F</mi><mo>∙</mo></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F_{\bullet}</annotation></semantics></math> is</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg(N_{i})=\sum_{j=1}^{\beta_{i}}a_{j}^{(i)}=(g-k-1)(k-2-i)\binom{k-2}{i-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>β</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></munderover><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg(N_{i})=\sum_{j=1}^{\beta_{i}}a_{j}^{(i)}=(g-k-1)(k-2-i)\binom{k-2}{i-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Since the rank and degree of the syzygy bundles <math id="S1.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S1.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> are known, the main object of investigation is the <em id="S1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">splitting type</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1.5</span></span><span id="S1.Thmdefinition5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">CE96</a>]</cite>, Casnati and Ekedahl generalized the relative canonical resolution to finite Gorenstein covers <math id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi:X\to Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi:X\to Y</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>.
They define a relative resolution of <math id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\subset\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E}_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\subset\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E}_{T})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{E}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{E}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is the
the Tschirnhausen bundle on <math id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> defined by short exact sequence</p>
<table id="S1.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0\to\mathscr{O}_{Y}\to\pi_{*}(\mathscr{O}_{X})\to\mathscr{E}_{T}^{\vee}\to 0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mi>T</mi><mo>∨</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0\to\mathscr{O}_{Y}\to\pi_{*}(\mathscr{O}_{X})\to\mathscr{E}_{T}^{\vee}\to 0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Note that for a cover <math id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="C\overset{k:1}{\longrightarrow}\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mover accent="true"><mo stretchy="false">⟶</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">k</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" mathsize="0.700em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow></mover><mi>ℙ</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\overset{k:1}{\longrightarrow}\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{E}_{T}=\mathscr{E}\otimes\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{E}_{T}=\mathscr{E}\otimes\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(2)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{E}</annotation></semantics></math> is the bundle associate to <math id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C,g^{1}_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C,g^{1}_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> as in Theorem <a href="#S1.Thmdefinition3" title="Theorem 1.3 ([], Corollary 4.4). ‣ 1 Relative Canonical Resolutions ‣ The relative canonical resolution: Macaulay2-package, experiments and conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.3</span></a>.
The twists and hence the splitting types of the syzygy bundles in a resolution of <math id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E}_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E}_{T})</annotation></semantics></math> also differ from the ones in the relative canonical resolution of <math id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset{\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset{\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Indeed, following the proof of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">CE96</a>, Step B, p. 445]</cite>, each twist in the <math id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th syzygy bundles in a resolution of <math id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E}_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E}_{T})</annotation></semantics></math> differs by exactly <math id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\cdot(i+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\cdot(i+1)</annotation></semantics></math> from the ones given in our definition.
Hence, we can deduce the degrees of the bundles in this relative resolution of <math id="S1.Thmdefinition5.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E}_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E}_{T})</annotation></semantics></math> from Proposition <a href="#S1.Thmdefinition4" title="Proposition 1.4 ([], Proposition 2.9). ‣ 1 Relative Canonical Resolutions ‣ The relative canonical resolution: Macaulay2-package, experiments and conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.4</span></a>. These degrees have also been computed directly in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">DP18</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1.6</span></span><span id="S1.Thmdefinition6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We say that a bundle on <math id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> of the form <math id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=\bigoplus_{j=1}^{\beta}\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(a_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>β</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=\bigoplus_{j=1}^{\beta}\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(a_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">balanced</em> if</p>
<table id="S1.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\max_{i,j}|a_{j}-a_{i}|\leq 1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max_{i,j}|a_{j}-a_{i}|\leq 1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Equivalently, the bundle <math id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is balanced if <math id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{1}(\mathbb{P}^{1},\mathscr{E}\!nd(N))=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.830em;" width="0.830em"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi></mpadded><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{1}(\mathbb{P}^{1},\mathscr{E}\!nd(N))=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
The relative canonical resolution is called balanced if
all bundles <math id="S1.Thmdefinition6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> occurring in the resolution are balanced.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1.7</span></span><span id="S1.Thmdefinition7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmdefinition7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The locus of curves inside <math id="S1.Thmdefinition7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{H}_{g,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{H}_{g,k}</annotation></semantics></math> which have a balanced relative canonical resolution forms an open subset of <math id="S1.Thmdefinition7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{H}_{g,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{H}_{g,k}</annotation></semantics></math> which might be empty.
Hence, to show the generic balancedness for fixed values <math id="S1.Thmdefinition7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,k)</annotation></semantics></math> it is sufficient to examine a single balanced example.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmdefinition8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1.8</span></span><span id="S1.Thmdefinition8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmdefinition8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmdefinition8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The scroll associated to a general element in <math id="S1.Thmdefinition8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{H}_{g,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{H}_{g,k}</annotation></semantics></math> is always balanced by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bal89</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Har81</a>]</cite>. The sublocus inside <math id="S1.Thmdefinition8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{H}_{g,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{H}_{g,k}</annotation></semantics></math> parametrizing covers such that the associated scroll is unbalanced defines a divisor on <math id="S1.Thmdefinition8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{H}_{g,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{H}_{g,k}</annotation></semantics></math> precisely if <math id="S1.Thmdefinition8.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is a multiple of <math id="S1.Thmdefinition8.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(k-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(k-1)</annotation></semantics></math>. This divisor is called the <em id="S1.Thmdefinition8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Maroni-divisor</em> (for more details on the Maroni-divisor see e.g. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">vdGK17</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">DP15</a>]</cite>). 
<br class="ltx_break">On the other hand, knowing the splitting type of the syzygy bundles in the relative canonical resolution for generic elements in <math id="S1.Thmdefinition8.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{H}_{g,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{H}_{g,k}</annotation></semantics></math> one can study the sublocus inside <math id="S1.Thmdefinition8.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{H}_{g,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{H}_{g,k}</annotation></semantics></math> consisting set-theoretically of curves for which a certain syzygy bundle has non-generic splitting type. This yields interesting subvarieties which also turn out to be divisors in some cases (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">DP18</a>]</cite>). Similar to the Koszul-divisor on the moduli space <math id="S1.Thmdefinition8.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{M}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℳ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{M}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>, the study of the divisors obtained from the relative canonical resolution sheds light on the global geometry of the Hurwitz space.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span><span id="S2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Macaulay2</span>-package</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Macaulay2</span>-Package <span id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RelativeCanonicalResolution.m2</span> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">GS</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">BH15a</a>]</cite>) includes various useful functions to do experiments with <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-gonal canonical curves and the relative canonical resolution of those curves.
We will briefly explain how functions in this package construct <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>-nodal <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-gonal canonical curves of genus <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The main idea is that we start with a rational normalization of the desired curve and a degree <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> map from the normalization to <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. In the next step we pairwise glue <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g</annotation></semantics></math> chosen points on the normalization. If <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is a line bundle of degree <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> on a <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math>-nodal curve <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> with rational normalization <math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu:\mathbb{P}\to C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu:\mathbb{P}\to C</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{L}</annotation></semantics></math> is given as <math id="S2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu(\mathscr{L})\cong\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ν</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu(\mathscr{L})\cong\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(k)</annotation></semantics></math> together with gluing data between the residue class fields</p>
<table id="S2.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{a_{i}}{b_{i}}:\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(k)\otimes\Bbbk(P_{i})\to\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(k)\otimes\Bbbk(Q_{i})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">𝕜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊗</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">𝕜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{a_{i}}{b_{i}}:\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(k)\otimes\Bbbk(P_{i})\to\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(k)\otimes\Bbbk(Q_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=\Bbbk[s,t]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">𝕜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=\Bbbk[s,t]</annotation></semantics></math> be the coordinate ring of <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. We start over by choosing two forms <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f,h\in S_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f,h\in S_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> of degree <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> points <math id="S2.p3.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R_{i}=(R_{i}^{(0)}:R_{i}^{(1)})\in\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{i}=(R_{i}^{(0)}:R_{i}^{(1)})\in\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> such that for all <math id="S2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=1,\dots,g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=1,\dots,g</annotation></semantics></math> the determinant</p>
<table id="S2.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\det\begin{pmatrix}f&amp;R_{i}^{(0)}\\
h&amp;R_{i}^{(1)}\end{pmatrix}=l_{i}^{0}\cdot l_{i}^{(1)}\cdot r_{i}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>f</mi></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>h</mi></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\det\begin{pmatrix}f&amp;R_{i}^{(0)}\\
h&amp;R_{i}^{(1)}\end{pmatrix}=l_{i}^{0}\cdot l_{i}^{(1)}\cdot r_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">has at least two linear factors <math id="S2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{i}^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{i}^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l_{i}^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l_{i}^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that this step might be hard to perform over a field <math id="S2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Bbbk" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">𝕜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Bbbk</annotation></semantics></math> of characteristic <math id="S2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math> and we therefore
work over a finite field.
We compute <math id="S2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g</annotation></semantics></math> points <math id="S2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{i}=V(l_{i}^{(0)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{i}=V(l_{i}^{(0)})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{i}=V(l_{i}^{(1)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{i}=V(l_{i}^{(1)})</annotation></semantics></math> as the vanishing loci of these linear forms. We want to define multipliers <math id="S2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a_{i},b_{i}\}_{i=1,\dots,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a_{i},b_{i}\}_{i=1,\dots,g}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S2.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}\cdot f(P_{i})=a_{i}\cdot h(Q_{i})\text{ and }b_{i}\cdot h(P_{i})=a_{i}\cdot f(Q_{i})\text{ for }i=1,\dots,g." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}\cdot f(P_{i})=a_{i}\cdot h(Q_{i})\text{ and }b_{i}\cdot h(P_{i})=a_{i}\cdot f(Q_{i})\text{ for }i=1,\dots,g.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">By construction, we can choose <math id="S2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{a_{i},b_{i}\}_{i=1,\dots,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{a_{i},b_{i}\}_{i=1,\dots,g}</annotation></semantics></math> to be <math id="S2.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{i}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{i}=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{i}=\frac{f(P_{i})}{f(Q_{i})}=\frac{h(P_{i})}{h(Q_{i})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{i}=\frac{f(P_{i})}{f(Q_{i})}=\frac{h(P_{i})}{h(Q_{i})}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If we define</p>
<table id="S2.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}:=\det\begin{pmatrix}s&amp;P_{i}^{(0)}\\
t&amp;P_{i}^{(1)}\end{pmatrix}\cdot\det\begin{pmatrix}s&amp;Q_{i}^{(0)}\\
t&amp;Q_{i}^{(1)}\end{pmatrix}\ \text{ for }i=1,\dots,g," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">:=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>s</mi></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>t</mi></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>P</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>⋅</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0.055em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">det</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mi>s</mi></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>t</mi></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}:=\det\begin{pmatrix}s&amp;P_{i}^{(0)}\\
t&amp;P_{i}^{(1)}\end{pmatrix}\cdot\det\begin{pmatrix}s&amp;Q_{i}^{(0)}\\
t&amp;Q_{i}^{(1)}\end{pmatrix}\ \text{ for }i=1,\dots,g,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p3.4" class="ltx_p">then a basis of <math id="S2.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}(C,\omega_{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}(C,\omega_{C})</annotation></semantics></math> can be identified with</p>
<table id="S2.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Bigg\{s_{j}:=\prod_{i=1,i\neq j}^{g}q_{i}\Bigg\}_{j=1,\dots,g}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">:=</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></mrow><mi>g</mi></munderover><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Bigg\{s_{j}:=\prod_{i=1,i\neq j}^{g}q_{i}\Bigg\}_{j=1,\dots,g}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p3.5" class="ltx_p">This basis <math id="S2.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{s_{j}\}_{j=1,\dots,g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{s_{j}\}_{j=1,\dots,g}</annotation></semantics></math> can furthermore be modified in such a way that the scroll defined by the line bundle of degree <math id="S2.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> will have a "normalized" form, i.e., the <math id="S2.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times(g-k+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times(g-k+1)</annotation></semantics></math> matrix defining the scroll will consist of blocks of the form</p>
<table id="S2.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{pmatrix}t_{i}&amp;t_{i+2}\\
t_{i+1}&amp;t_{i+3}\end{pmatrix}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{pmatrix}t_{i}&amp;t_{i+2}\\
t_{i+1}&amp;t_{i+3}\end{pmatrix},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p3.6" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=\Bbbk[t_{0},\dots,t_{g-1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">𝕜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=\Bbbk[t_{0},\dots,t_{g-1}]</annotation></semantics></math> is the coordinate ring of <math id="S2.p3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}^{g-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}^{g-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In the package <span id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RelativeCanonicalResolution</span> we also provide a function which
describes the generators of <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> in terms of elements of the Cox ring of the scroll <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There is an explicit identification</p>
<table id="S2.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{0}({\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})},\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})}(aH+bR))\cong H^{0}(\mathbb{P}^{1},(S_{a}\mathscr{E})(b))\text{ for }a\geq 0," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{0}({\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})},\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})}(aH+bR))\cong H^{0}(\mathbb{P}^{1},(S_{a}\mathscr{E})(b))\text{ for }a\geq 0,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{a}\mathscr{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{a}\mathscr{E}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a^{th}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a^{th}</annotation></semantics></math> symmetric power of the vector bundle <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{E}</annotation></semantics></math> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sch86</a>, (1.3)]</cite>). This gives a description of the coordinate ring</p>
<table id="S2.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{{\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})}}=\bigoplus_{a,b\in\mathbb{Z}}H^{0}({\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})},\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})}(aH+bR))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{{\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})}}=\bigoplus_{a,b\in\mathbb{Z}}H^{0}({\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})},\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})}(aH+bR))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">of <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})}</annotation></semantics></math> as the Cox ring <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Bbbk[v,w,\varphi_{0},\dots,\varphi_{d-1}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">𝕜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Bbbk[v,w,\varphi_{0},\dots,\varphi_{d-1}]</annotation></semantics></math> equipped with bigrading <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg v=\deg w=(1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg v=\deg w=(1,0)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmdefinition1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\deg\varphi_{i}=(e_{1}-e_{i+1},1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>φ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\deg\varphi_{i}=(e_{1}-e_{i+1},1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Finally the relative canonical resolution of <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset{\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset{\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})}</annotation></semantics></math> can be computed by successively picking syzygies in correct degrees.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compute a nodal <math id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math>-gonal canonical curve of genus <math id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9</annotation></semantics></math>.</p><pre id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.2" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">
i1 : loadPackage("RelativeCanonicalResolution")
i2 : g=9; -- the genus
i3 : k=6; -- the degree of the pencil
i4 : n=10000; -- characteristic: next prime number after n
i5 : Ican=canCurveWithFixedScroll(g,k,n); -- the canonical curve
i6 : (dim Ican,genus Ican, degree Ican)
o6 = (2, 9, 16)
i7 : betti(res(Ican,DegreeLimit=&gt;1))
            0  1  2  3
o7 = total: 1 15 35 21
         0: 1  .  .  .
         1: . 21 64 70
</pre>
<p id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Next we compute the ideal of <math id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> inside the Cox ring of the scroll <math id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbb{P}(\mathscr{E})}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p><pre id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.4" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">
i8 : Jcan=curveOnScroll(Ican,g,k); -- the curve inside the scroll
i9 : RX=ring Jcan; -- the bigraded Cox ring of the scroll
       ZZ
o9 = -----[pp , pp , pp , pp , pp , v,w]
     10007   0    1    2    3    4
</pre>
<p id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.5" class="ltx_p">We compute the relative canonical resolution:</p><pre id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.6" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">¯
i10 : T=ring Ican; -- the canonical ring
i11 : H=basis({1,1},RX); -- a basis of Hˆ0(PE, OO_PE(H))
i12 : phi=map(RX,T,H)
i13 : Ican==preimage_phi(Jcan)
o13 = true
i14 : lengthRes=2; -- a lengthlimit for the resolution on the scroll
</pre>
<p id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p1.7" class="ltx_p">With respect to the total degree, the Betti table of the relative canonical resolution has the following form:</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p2" class="ltx_para"><pre id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p2.1" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">-- the relative canonical resolution:
i15 : betti(resX=resCurveOnScroll(Jcan,g,lengthRes))
             0 1  2 3 4
o15 = total: 1 9 16 9 1
          0: 1 .  . . .
          1: . .  . . .
          2: . 6  2 . .
          3: . 3 12 3 .
          4: . .  2 6 .
          5: . .  . . 1
</pre>
<p id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The scroll cut out by the <math id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{1}_{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mn>6</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{1}_{6}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> has the following normalized determinantal representation:</p><pre id="S2.Thmdefinition2.p2.3" class="ltx_verbatim ltx_font_typewriter">
i16 : X=preimage_phi(ideal 0_RX); -- the ideal of the scroll
i17 : repX=matrix{{t_0,t_2,t_4,t_6},{t_1,t_3,t_5,t_7}} ¯
o17 = | t_0 t_2 t_4 t_6 |
      | t_1 t_3 t_5 t_7 |
i18 : minors(2,repX)==X
o18 = true
</pre>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmdefinition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmdefinition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmdefinition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmdefinition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By <span id="S2.Thmdefinition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">o15</span>, we see that the second syzygy bundle <math id="S2.Thmdefinition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is unbalanced in our example.
Although this single example does not show the generic unbalancedness for this case, one can show that this is indeed the generic form (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">BH17</a>]</cite>).</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Experiments and conjectures</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Database of experiments</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Using our <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Macaulay2</span>-package <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RelativeCanonicalResolution.m2</span> we have computed the relative canonical resolution for various cases. For non-hyperelliptic, generic curves of genus <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\leq 23" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>23</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\leq 23</annotation></semantics></math> with a pencil of degree <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\leq k\leq\min\{g-1,14\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\leq k\leq\min\{g-1,14\}</annotation></semantics></math>, all expected Betti tables are listed on the following webpage</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<div id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:6.5pt;vertical-align:-1.6pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-72.3pt,1.4pt) scale(0.704567000133809,0.704567000133809) ;">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><a target="_blank" href="https://www.math.uni-sb.de/ag/schreyer/images/data/computeralgebra/relcanres/html/index.html" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://www.math.uni-sb.de/ag/schreyer/images/data/computeralgebra/relcanres/html/index.html</a></p>
</span></div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The webpage was set up with the help of Sascha Blug.
All the experiments which led to Betti tables in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">BBH18</a>]</cite> were performed over a finite field. If the examples for certain values <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,k)</annotation></semantics></math> yield a balanced relative canonical resolution, then by semi-continuity one can conclude that this is indeed the general behavior (even for complex algebraic curves).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Since changing the characteristic for the unbalanced cases did not change the shape of the Betti tables, we believe that the Betti tables in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">BBH18</a>]</cite> reflect the generic behavior. In general, we do not have a proof of this statement. However, the (un-)balancedness has been proved for the first bundle <math id="S3.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> in some cases (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">BH15b</a>]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">BP15</a>]</cite>). For several cases our examples lead to the conjecture, that certain higher syzygy bundles in the relative canonical resolution are unbalanced. Most of these cases remain rather mysterious.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Syzygy divisors on Hurwitz spaces</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Deopurkar and Patel used the relative canonical resolution to describe new effective divisors on the Hurwitz scheme <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{H}_{g,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{H}_{g,k}</annotation></semantics></math>. If the degree <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> divides <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g-1</annotation></semantics></math>,
it is shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">BP15</a>]</cite> that the relative canonical resolution for a generic element in <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{H}_{g,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{H}_{g,k}</annotation></semantics></math> is totally balanced and hence, the locus <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, corresponding set-theoretically to covers in <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{H}_{g,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{H}_{g,k}</annotation></semantics></math> for which the <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th syzygy bundle <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is unbalanced, has expected codimension one.
In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">DP18</a>]</cite> the authors give these syzygy divisor <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{1},\dots,\mu_{k-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{1},\dots,\mu_{k-3}</annotation></semantics></math> a scheme structure and compute their classes in a partial compactification of the Hurwitz scheme <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathscr{H}}_{g,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathscr{H}}_{g,k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In their main theorem, they represent the divisor classes <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mu_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mu_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math> in terms of certain tautological classes <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa,\zeta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa,\zeta</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">DP18</a>, §2]</cite> for the precise definition of those classes).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">DP18</a>, Theorem 1.1]</cite>)<span id="S3.Thmdefinition1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> divides <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> be an integer with <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq k-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq k-3</annotation></semantics></math>. The locus <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}\subset\widetilde{\mathscr{H}}_{g,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}\subset\widetilde{\mathscr{H}}_{g,k}</annotation></semantics></math> is an effective divisor whose class in <math id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{{Pic}}_{\mathbb{Q}}(\widetilde{\mathscr{H}}_{g,k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>Pic</mtext><mi>ℚ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{{Pic}}_{\mathbb{Q}}(\widetilde{\mathscr{H}}_{g,k})</annotation></semantics></math> is given by</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mu_{i}]=A_{i}\cdot\bigg(6(gk-6g+k+6)\cdot\zeta-k(k-12)\cdot\kappa-k^{2}\cdot\delta\bigg)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>ζ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mu_{i}]=A_{i}\cdot\bigg(6(gk-6g+k+6)\cdot\zeta-k(k-12)\cdot\kappa-k^{2}\cdot\delta\bigg),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}=\frac{(k-2)(k-3)}{6(i+1)(k-i-1)}\cdot\binom{k-4}{i-1}^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}=\frac{(k-2)(k-3)}{6(i+1)(k-i-1)}\cdot\binom{k-4}{i-1}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Note that all the classes <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mu_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mu_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math> are proportional. The same phenomenon appears for classes of divisorial Brill–Noether loci in the moduli space <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathscr{M}}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathscr{M}}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For the divisorial Brill–Noether classes it is known that these classes are supported on different sets and in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">DP18</a>]</cite> the authors conjecture that this also happens for the syzygy divisors on <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{H}_{g,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{H}_{g,k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We come to a different conclusion.
Computing various examples of curves and their relative canonical resolution for <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g,k)\in\{(6,13),(7,15),(8,17),(6,19)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>13</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>15</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>17</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>19</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g,k)\in\{(6,13),(7,15),(8,17),(6,19)\}</annotation></semantics></math> over a field of small characteristic <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\leq 500" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>500</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\leq 500</annotation></semantics></math> we found the following pattern which we conjecture to be true in general.
Note that computing random examples, the probability to end up in a certain codimension one locus is roughly <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{1}{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>p</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{1}{p}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmconjecture1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmconjecture1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 1</span></span><span id="Thmconjecture1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmconjecture1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmconjecture1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconjecture1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n,k</annotation></semantics></math> be integers and <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g-1=n\cdot k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g-1=n\cdot k</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> be an integer with <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq k-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq k-3</annotation></semantics></math>. For a general element <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(C,g^{1}_{k})\in\mu_{i}\subset\widetilde{\mathscr{H}}_{g,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊂</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(C,g^{1}_{k})\in\mu_{i}\subset\widetilde{\mathscr{H}}_{g,k}</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th syzygy bundle in the relative canonical resolution of <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq j\leq k-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq j\leq k-3</annotation></semantics></math>. Then all <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> are unbalanced and the splitting type of <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{j}=\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}\big((n-1)(j+1)-1\big)^{\oplus\binom{k-4}{j-1}}\bigoplus\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}\big((n-1)(j+1)\big)^{\text{{rk}}N_{j}-2\cdot\binom{k-4}{j-1}}\bigoplus\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}\big((n-1)(j+1)+1\big)^{\oplus\binom{k-4}{j-1}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>rk</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{j}=\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}\big((n-1)(j+1)-1\big)^{\oplus\binom{k-4}{j-1}}\bigoplus\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}\big((n-1)(j+1)\big)^{\text{{rk}}N_{j}-2\cdot\binom{k-4}{j-1}}\bigoplus\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}\big((n-1)(j+1)+1\big)^{\oplus\binom{k-4}{j-1}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmconjecture1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconjecture1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular, all the effective divisors <math id="Thmconjecture1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are supported on the same set.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">One can easily check that the number <math id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in Theorem <a href="#S3.Thmdefinition1" title="Theorem 3.1 ([, Theorem 1.1]). ‣ 3.2 Syzygy divisors on Hurwitz spaces ‣ 3 Experiments and conjectures ‣ The relative canonical resolution: Macaulay2-package, experiments and conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> is precisely</p>
<table id="S3.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}=\frac{1}{6k}\cdot\text{{rk}}N_{i}\cdot\binom{k-4}{i-1}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mtext>rk</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}=\frac{1}{6k}\cdot\text{{rk}}N_{i}\cdot\binom{k-4}{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">The conjecture above predicts that the factor <math id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{k-4}{i-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{k-4}{i-1}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> also measures the unbalancedness of the bundle <math id="S3.Thmdefinition2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmdefinition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g-1)\neq n\cdot k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g-1)\neq n\cdot k</annotation></semantics></math> then one can still consider the jumping loci set-theoretically defined as the subset of <math id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathscr{H}}_{g,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℋ</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathscr{H}}_{g,k}</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of covers such that the <math id="S3.Thmdefinition3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th syzygy bundle in the relative canonical resolution does not have generic splitting type. Similarly to the divisorial case one could ask if all those loci are supported on the same set. Experiments using our package <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">BH15a</a>]</cite> show that there are several examples where these jumping loci have different support.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Further conjectures</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We state several conjectures concerning the shape of relative canonical resolutions. This has partly also been discussed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">BH15b</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmconjecture2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmconjecture2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 2</span></span><span id="Thmconjecture2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmconjecture2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmconjecture2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconjecture2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\subset\mathbb{P}^{g-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⊂</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\subset\mathbb{P}^{g-1}</annotation></semantics></math> be a general canonical curve and let <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> be a positive integer such that <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho:=\rho(g,k,1)\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho:=\rho(g,k,1)\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{1}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{1}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be a general pencil in <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{1}_{k}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{1}_{k}(C)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then for bundles <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}=\bigoplus\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(a_{j}^{(i)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>⨁</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}=\bigoplus\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(a_{j}^{(i)})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=2,\dots,\big\lceil\frac{k-3}{2}\big\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=2,\dots,\big\lceil\frac{k-3}{2}\big\rceil</annotation></semantics></math> there is the following bound</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{j,l}\big|a_{j}^{(i)}-a_{l}^{(i)}\big|\leq\min\{g-k-1,i+1\}\vskip-2.84526pt." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{j,l}\big|a_{j}^{(i)}-a_{l}^{(i)}\big|\leq\min\{g-k-1,i+1\}\vskip-2.84526pt.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmconjecture2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconjecture2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">This bound is furthermore sharp in the following sense. Given two integers <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\leq i\leq\lceil\frac{k-3}{2}\rceil" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⌈</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">⌉</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\leq i\leq\lceil\frac{k-3}{2}\rceil</annotation></semantics></math>, there exists an integer <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> such that the general canonical curve <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> of genus <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> has an <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th syzygy bundle <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in the relative canonical resolution, associated to a general pencil in <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{1}_{k}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{1}_{k}(C)</annotation></semantics></math>, which satisfies <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="max_{j,l}|a_{j}^{(i)}-a_{l}^{(i)}|=\min\{g-k-1,i+1\}\vskip-2.84526pt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mi>l</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">max_{j,l}|a_{j}^{(i)}-a_{l}^{(i)}|=\min\{g-k-1,i+1\}\vskip-2.84526pt</annotation></semantics></math>.
In particular, if <math id="Thmconjecture2.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g-k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g-k=2</annotation></semantics></math>, the relative canonical resolution is balanced.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmdefinition4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmdefinition4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmdefinition4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmdefinition4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The above conjecture in the case <math id="S3.Thmdefinition4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g-k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g-k=2</annotation></semantics></math> says that the bundles in the relative canonical resolution are of the following form:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="N_{i}=\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}^{\oplus i\cdot\binom{g-4}{i+1}}\bigoplus\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(1)^{\oplus(g-4-i)\cdot\binom{g-4}{g-3-i}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></msubsup><mo movablelimits="false">⨁</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒪</mi><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></msub><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{i}=\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}^{\oplus i\cdot\binom{g-4}{i+1}}\bigoplus\mathscr{O}_{\mathbb{P}^{1}}(1)^{\oplus(g-4-i)\cdot\binom{g-4}{g-3-i}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmdefinition4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Note that the Betti numbers <math id="S3.Thmdefinition4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\cdot\binom{k-2}{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\cdot\binom{k-2}{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> appearing in the conjecture are the Betti numbers of a rational normal curve of degree <math id="S3.Thmdefinition4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k-2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We also verified the following Conjecture <a href="#Thmconjecture3" title="Conjecture 3. ‣ 3.3 Further conjectures ‣ 3 Experiments and conjectures ‣ The relative canonical resolution: Macaulay2-package, experiments and conjectures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> for <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\leq 23" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>23</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\leq 23</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmconjecture3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_conjecture">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmconjecture3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conjecture 3</span></span><span id="Thmconjecture3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmconjecture3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmconjecture3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmconjecture3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a general cover <math id="Thmconjecture3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\to\mathbb{P}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℙ</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\to\mathbb{P}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Thmconjecture3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{H}_{g,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">ℋ</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{H}_{g,k}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmconjecture3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho(g,k,1)\leq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho(g,k,1)\leq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, the bundle <math id="Thmconjecture3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is balanced.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Acknowledgement</h4>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We would like to thank Sascha Blug for setting up the webpage <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">BBH18</a>]</cite> which displays all the experimental data. This work is a contribution to the Project 1.7 of the SFB-TRR 195 ”Symbolic Tools in Mathematics and their Application“ of the German Research Foundation (DFG).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
